\import{ftip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{Mechanism families and capability bottlenecks}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{architecture}
\tag{mechanism}
\tag{optional-refinement}

\p{This map asks which \newvocab{mechanism family} changes a declared
capability bottleneck and what evidence would show that change. It is a
comparison aid, not a ranking of architectures. The shared model, task,
intervention, cost and evaluation interfaces remain the conditions of every
claim. A source description, a measured result and a transfer claim are kept
separate.}

\p{The five capability dimensions are \newvocab{state access}, the
information that can remain available across tokens, steps or external memory
operations; \newvocab{adaptive computation}, conditional allocation of depth,
recurrence, search or stopping together with its charged cost;
\newvocab{representation and abstraction}, the available representation,
composition or predictive state; \newvocab{plasticity}, the changes reachable
by a declared objective, optimizer, parameter tying and post-training recipe;
and \newvocab{portability}, survival of a result when the model family, task,
scale or deployment regime changes. This is distinct from
\vocabk{transfer}{ftip-00NR}, which evaluates a frozen artifact on new
problems under a common deployment budget. They are coordinates for a
capability record, not a single intelligence scalar.}

\p{A \newvocab{bottleneck} is the declared resource or interface condition
that limits one of these dimensions in a comparison. Cost, numerical
stability and \newvocab{monitorability} remain evaluation coordinates: the
ability to observe the relevant state, compute, update or failure signals
under the study protocol. A \vocabk{matched-compute comparison}{ftip-00JU} fixes the
cost accounting before comparing outcomes; its protocol and the architecture
\vocabk{frontier}{ftip-00JN} remain authoritative elsewhere in this chapter.}

\p{The default \newvocab{core panel} contains mechanism families that alter
the sequence computation or its state interface. Existing Transformer
notation and the decoder-only reference are in \ref{ftip-000K}; the entries
below identify the question each family makes testable.}

\ol{
  \li{\strong{Transformer reference.} Attention exposes token-indexed
  content access through the declared context and serves as the baseline for
  the \vocabk{matched-compute comparison}{ftip-00JU} and interface comparisons.}
  \li{\strong{Selective state-space models.} The
  [Mamba construction, Sections 3.1--3.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2312.00752v2#S3)
  makes the state-space parameters input-dependent and computes the resulting
  time-varying recurrence with a hardware-aware scan. The testable bottleneck
  is how selective compression trades long-context access and throughput
  against retrieval and portability boundaries; a source mechanism does not
  settle that tradeoff.}
  \li{\strong{Linear attention.} The
  [linear-attention formulation, Sections 3.2--3.3](https://arxiv.org/html/2006.16236v3#S3)
  uses a feature-map factorization and associative prefix summaries rather
  than the full softmax attention matrix. The comparison asks which retrieval
  and memory tasks preserve their evaluation under the changed state
  interface; it does not identify every hybrid or recurrent model with pure
  linear attention.}
  \li{\strong{Internal-tick and looped models.} The
  [Continuous Thought Machine, Sections 3.1--3.5](https://arxiv.org/html/2505.05522v4#S3)
  uses a discrete internal tick dimension, private neuron-level models that
  process activation histories, synchronization as a latent representation,
  and standard cross-attention to the input features. Its loss selects
  minimum-loss and maximum-certainty ticks, making variable internal compute
  a testable mechanism. The looped computations in \ref{ftip-00KG} are a
  separate family; neither is a continuous-time claim, and every extra tick
  or loop must be charged in the comparison.}
  \li{\strong{Recurrent or compressed-state mechanisms.} The
  [Dragon Hatchling / BDH proposal, Section 3.2](https://arxiv.org/html/2509.26507v1#S3.SS2)
  defines a tensor-friendly BDH-GPU state-space system whose attention state
  admits a reduced representation, alongside local graph dynamics. Its open
  comparison is whether this state interface supports the target tasks and
  stable credit assignment at a declared budget; the proposal is not a
  production result.}
}

\p{The \newvocab{adjunct panel} changes an objective, temporal law or
function-space bias without being interchangeable with the core architecture
families. [I-JEPA, Section 3](https://arxiv.org/html/2301.08243v3#S3) is an
encoder/predictor training architecture as well as an embedding-space
prediction objective: its context, target and predictor use ViT backbones,
the target encoder is updated by exponential moving average, and masked target
blocks are predicted from a context block. That predictive objective leaves a
specific representation limit: retaining or recovering the latent state does
not by itself identify which directions are invariant and which vary across
matched observations. [SplitJEPA, Sections 3.1--3.4](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.12349v1#S3)
(the [project overview](https://splitjepa.github.io/)) makes the boundary
explicit—predictive recovery can leave a global orthogonal mixing—and resolves
it under [stationary Gaussian predictive dynamics](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.12349v1#S2),
a [full-rank second moment of variant differences across invariant
correspondences](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.12349v1#S3.SS2), and
[representation normalization](https://arxiv.org/html/2610.12349v1#S3.SS4),
by identifying invariant and variant subspaces up to within-block isometries
without an observation decoder.
Here SplitJEPA is a structured JEPA adjunct and a conditional identifiability
result, not a general guarantee for I-JEPA. [Liquid time-constant
networks, Sections 1--2](https://arxiv.org/html/2006.04439v4#S2) supply
input-dependent continuous dynamics and a numerical ODE-solver interface; the
[Neural Operator formulation, Sections 1 and 3](https://arxiv.org/html/2108.08481v6#S3)
supplies a function-space lens with discretization-invariant operator layers.
The [Zoology study, Sections 3--5](https://arxiv.org/html/2312.04927v1#S3)
provides controlled recall analyses of sequence mixers. These sources
motivate hypotheses and controls; they do not establish a common capability
ordering or make an adjunct a replacement architecture. The
[JEPA comparability interface](ftip-00KA) specifies fields that must be fixed
for a JEPA-like comparison; it is not the source of I-JEPA's architecture.
Combining CTM's internal dynamics with I-JEPA's encoder/predictor package is a
proposed joint intervention, not an established result, and would require
separate attribution of both components.}

\p{For each panel row, a study should name the changed state or compute
  interface, the bottleneck dimension, the task and intervention, the evidence
  type, and the transport boundary. Existing \vocabk{architecture-indexed ceilings
and matched compute}{ftip-00JF}, published paired measurements
(\ref{ftip-00KU} and \ref{ftip-00L4}), conditional frontiers
(\ref{ftip-00LH}) and study design (\ref{ftip-00LS}) provide those controls.
Blank, incompatible and unmeasured cells remain findings rather than scores
to be filled by intuition.}
